  "worked_solution": "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand ${e}^{-|x|}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-|x|}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
